## PART B — PROJECT 1: StatusPing

### Project Identity

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Name** | StatusPing |
| **One-liner** | Open-source status page and uptime monitor you can self-host or use as a service |
| **Real problem** | Solo developers and small teams running side-projects, APIs, or micro-SaaS products need to communicate uptime to users but existing solutions (Statuspage.io, BetterUptime) are either expensive ($29+/mo), overly complex, or lock you into their infrastructure |
| **Target users** | Indie hackers, solo SaaS founders, small dev teams, open-source project maintainers |
| **Why it matters** | When your service goes down, users need a single reliable URL to check. Without a status page, founders get flooded with support emails. Research shows the "status page as a service" niche for micro-SaaS is underserved — most tools target enterprise customers |
| **Primary use case** | A founder creates a StatusPing account, adds their endpoints (URLs, ports), gets a public status page at `status.myapp.com`, and receives alerts when things break |
| **Secondary use cases** | Incident timeline with manual updates; scheduled maintenance announcements; embeddable status badge |
| **Beyond CRUD** | Background job scheduling (health checks every N seconds), time-series data storage for uptime history, multi-tenant data isolation, public vs. private page access, webhook/notification integrations |

### Product Definition (Summary)

- **User journey:** Sign up → Add monitors (URL, interval, expected status) → Get public status page URL → Receive alerts on downtime → Post incident updates → Users see real-time status
- **Core workflow:** Background scheduler pings endpoints → stores results → computes uptime % → renders public page → sends notifications on state change
- **MVP scope:** User auth, CRUD monitors, background health checks, public status page, uptime history (90 days), email alerts on downtime, incident management (create/update/resolve)
- **Post-MVP:** Custom domains, Slack/Discord webhook notifications, team members, API access, embeddable badge
- **Excluded from MVP:** SMS alerts, multi-region checks, complex escalation policies

### Technical Design (Summary)

| Layer | Choice | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| **Frontend** | Next.js (React) | SSR for public status pages (SEO), dashboard for authenticated users |
| **Backend** | Python (FastAPI) | REST API, background task scheduling, async HTTP checks |
| **Database** | PostgreSQL | Relational data (users, monitors, incidents), time-series-like check results with partitioning |
| **Auth** | Email/password with bcrypt + JWT | Standard web auth, no OAuth complexity needed for MVP |
| **Background jobs** | APScheduler or Celery with in-process scheduler | Periodic health checks; in-process for MVP, Celery for scale |
| **Notifications** | SMTP (email) | MVP notification channel |
| **Deployment** | Railway (backend + DB) + Vercel (frontend) | Free tiers, simple deployment |
| **Testing** | pytest + httpx (API), Jest + React Testing Library (frontend) | Standard stacks |
| **CI** | GitHub Actions | Lint, test, type-check, build |

### Architecture Summary

```
Public User                    Authenticated User
    │                               │
    ▼                               ▼
Public Status Page (Next.js)   Dashboard (Next.js)
    │                               │
    └──────────┬────────────────────┘
               ▼
         FastAPI REST API
          ├── Auth Service
          ├── Monitor Service
          ├── Incident Service
          ├── Check Scheduler ──→ HTTP/TCP checks ──→ Target endpoints
