## PART D — PROJECT 3: ClipVault

### Project Identity

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Name** | ClipVault |
| **One-liner** | Browser extension that saves and organizes code snippets from any webpage with context |
| **Real problem** | Developers constantly copy code from Stack Overflow, documentation, blog posts, and GitHub. These snippets end up scattered across text files, Slack messages, and forgotten browser tabs. Research shows "tab chaos" and context-switching are top developer productivity killers. Existing bookmark managers save pages, not the specific code block with its surrounding context |
| **Target users** | Software developers who read documentation and technical content in the browser daily |
| **Why it matters** | The gap between "reading code on the web" and "using it in your project" involves unnecessary friction — copying, switching apps, pasting, losing the source URL. Developers need a way to capture snippets with provenance |
| **Primary use case** | Developer highlights a code block on a docs page → right-clicks "Save to ClipVault" → snippet is saved with source URL, language, tags, and surrounding context → later searches their vault to find it |
| **Secondary use cases** | Browse saved snippets in extension popup; copy snippets with one click; filter by language/tag; export collection; share snippet with permalink |
| **Beyond CRUD** | Content script DOM interaction, browser storage with sync, context menu API, code language detection, search/filter in popup, backend sync for cross-device access |

### Product Definition (Summary)

- **User journey:** Install extension → browse normally → highlight code → right-click "Save to ClipVault" → snippet saved with metadata → click extension icon to browse/search vault → copy snippet to clipboard
- **Core workflow:** Content script detects selection → extracts code + metadata (URL, title, language) → saves to browser storage → popup provides searchable interface → optional backend sync
- **MVP scope:**
  - Chrome extension (Manifest V3)
  - Right-click context menu to save selected text as snippet
  - Auto-detect programming language from page context
  - Capture source URL, page title, timestamp
  - Popup UI: list, search, filter by language, copy to clipboard
  - Local browser storage (chrome.storage.local)
  - Tag snippets manually
  - Export as JSON
- **Post-MVP:** Backend sync service (FastAPI), cross-device sync, Firefox support, syntax highlighting in popup, snippet sharing via public links
- **Excluded from MVP:** AI summarization, collaborative collections, IDE integration

### Technical Design (Summary)

| Layer | Choice | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| **Extension platform** | Chrome (Manifest V3) | Largest market share, MV3 is current standard |
| **Content script** | Vanilla JS | Lightweight, no framework needed for DOM interaction |
| **Popup UI** | HTML + CSS + vanilla JS | Small UI, framework overhead not justified |
| **Storage (MVP)** | chrome.storage.local | No backend needed for MVP, 10MB limit sufficient |
| **Backend (post-MVP)** | FastAPI (Python) | Sync service for cross-device |
| **Language detection** | Heuristic: parent element classes, page URL patterns, keyword analysis | Deterministic approach, no AI needed |
| **Testing** | Jest (unit tests for logic), manual testing for extension | Extension testing is inherently integration-heavy |
| **CI** | GitHub Actions | Lint, test, build zip for Chrome Web Store |

### Architecture Summary

```
Web Page (any)
    │
    ▼
Content Script (injected)
    ├── Listens for context menu click
    ├── Extracts selected text
    ├── Captures metadata (URL, title, code language)
    └── Sends message to Service Worker
         │
         ▼
Service Worker (background)
    ├── Receives snippet data
    ├── Generates ID, timestamp
    ├── Stores in chrome.storage.local
    └── Updates badge count
         │
         ▼
Popup (browser action)
    ├── Reads from chrome.storage.local
    ├── Search/filter interface
    ├── Copy to clipboard
    ├── Tag management
    └── Export (JSON)

Permissions: contextMenus, storage, activeTab
No network requests in MVP. Minimal permissions.
```

### Key Engineering Skills Demonstrated

- **Browser APIs:** Manifest V3, content scripts, service workers, context menus, storage API
- **Privacy:** Minimal permissions, no data transmission in MVP, clear data model
- **Frontend:** Popup UI with search/filter, responsive design in constrained space
- **Architecture:** Message passing between content script ↔ service worker ↔ popup
- **Packaging:** Chrome Web Store submission, versioning
- **Testing:** Unit tests for data logic, integration considerations for browser APIs
- **Security:** Content Security Policy, input sanitization, XSS prevention in popup

